course: dictionary
lesson: 9350
title: Splay: spread wide
level: primary-4
stars: 0
explain: Some words describe how something looks or moves, and «splay» is one of them. To splay things is to spread them wide apart, like your fingers on a table. Look at the words around it for clues: «splayed legs» and «splay your fingers» both show things pushed apart.
explain: Picture the sentence and see if your meaning fits. «A frog sat on the log with its legs splayed.» The frog's legs are not held together; they are spread out wide. Swap in "spread out wide" and check that it still makes sense.
explain: Splay can also mean turned outward. In «A toothbrush with bent, splayed bristles does not clean well», the bristles point out in different directions instead of standing neat and straight. That is why the toothbrush needs changing.
fact: Splay means to spread things wide apart.
fact: «Splayed» means spread out wide or turned outward.
fact: You can splay your fingers, your legs or your feet.
fact: «To drink, a giraffe must splay its front legs and bend low.»
fact: Splaying its legs to drink leaves a giraffe open to lions, so it often gets water from leaves instead.
exercise: «She splayed her fingers wide on the piano keys.» What does splayed mean? | Closed into a tight fist | *Spread out wide | Washed clean | Hidden under her coat
exercise: «To drink, a giraffe must splay its front legs and bend low.» What does splay mean here? | To run very fast | *To spread the legs wide apart | To jump up high | To hide the legs under its body
exercise: Why do climbers sometimes splay their feet or hands? | To hide from other climbers | To wash the rock clean | *To keep their balance | To make themselves go faster
test: «The cat lay splayed in the sun, with all four legs stretched out.» Which word tells you the cat was spread out? | four | sun | cat | *splayed
test: Which picture matches «splayed legs»? | Legs crossed tightly at the ankles | Legs folded neatly under a chair | Legs tucked up close to the chest | *Legs spread wide apart
exam: Which sentence uses splay correctly? | *She splayed her toes to grip the wet sand. | She splayed a sandwich for her lunch. | The bell splayed loudly across the playground. | He splayed his homework neatly into his bag.
